WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.759
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:10.929
When we look at the cover page of a magazine,
we often see lots of well-made, but also idealized

00:00:10.929 --> 00:00:13.099
photos of people.

00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:17.350
Idealized here means that the photographer
made them in a studio, where they can add

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:22.460
or remove light sources and move them around
to bring out the best from their models.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:27.150
But most photos are not made in the studio,
they are made out there in the wild where

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.970
the lighting is what it is, and we can’t
control it too much.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:36.520
So, with that, today, our question is what
if we could change the lighting after the

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.400
photo has been made?

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.980
This work proposes a cool technique to perform
exactly that by enabling us to edit the shadows

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.900
on a portrait photo that we would normally
think of deleting.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:52.410
Many of these have to do with the presence
of shadows, and you can see here that we can

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:56.170
really edit these after the photo has been
taken.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:01.230
However, before we start taking a closer look
at the editing process, we have to note that

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.510
there are different kinds of shadows.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:09.960
One, there are shadows cast on us by external
objects, let’s call those foreign shadows,

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.340
and there is self-shadowing, which comes from
the model’s own facial features.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.590
Let’s call those facial shadows.

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:20.790
So why divide them into two classes?

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:26.600
Simple, because we typically seek to remove
foreign shadows, and edit facial shadows.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.580
The removal part can be done with a learning
algorithm, provided that we can teach it with

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.070
a lot of training data.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:36.900
Let’s think about ways to synthesize such
a large dataset!

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.330
Let’s start with the foreign shadows.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:44.840
We need image pairs of test subjects with
and without shadows to have the neural network

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.000
learn about their relations.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.880
Since removing shadows is difficult without
further interfering with the image, the authors

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.800
opted to do it the other way around.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.060
In other words, they take a clean photo of
the subject, that’s the one without the

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:03.200
shadows, and then, and add shadows to it algorithmically.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.530
Very cool!

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:10.700
And, the results are not bad at all, and get
this, they even accounted for subsurface scattering,

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:13.760
which is the scattering of light under our
skin.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.569
That makes a great deal of a difference.

00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:20.830
This is a reference from a paper we wrote
with scientists at the University of Zaragoza

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:26.060
and the Activision Blizzard company to add
this beautiful effect to their games.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:33.660
Here is a shadow edge without subsurface scattering,
quite dark, and with subsurface scattering,

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.300
you see this beautiful glowing effect.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:41.690
Subsurface scattering indeed makes a great
deal of difference around hard shadow edges,

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:46.170
so huge thumbs up for the authors for including
an approximation of that.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:50.660
However, the synthesized photos are still
a little suspect.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.400
We can still tell that they are synthesized.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.540
And that is kind of the point.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.370
Our question is “can the neural network
still learn the difference between a clean

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:03.610
and a shadowy photo” despite all this?

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:09.830
As you see, the problem is not easy - previous
methods did not do too well on these examples

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:13.590
when you compare them to the reference solution.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.720
And let’s see this new method.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:19.510
Wow, I can hardly believe my eyes.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:20.510
Nearly perfect.

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:25.770
And it did learn all this on not real, but
synthetic images.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:29.020
And believe it or not, this was only the simpler
part.

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:30.459
Now comes the hard part.

00:03:30.459 --> 00:03:34.860
Let’s look at how well it performs at editing
the facial shadows!

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:40.750
We can pretend to edit both the size and the
intensity of these light sources.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:45.890
The goal is to have a little more control
over the shadows in these photos, but, whatever

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:50.270
we do with them, the outputs still have to
remain realistic.

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:52.940
Here are the before and after results.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:57.830
The facial shadows have been weakened, and
depending on our artistic choices, we can

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.460
also soften the image a great deal.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.150
Absolutely amazing.

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:07.769
As a result, we now have a two-step algorithm,
that first, removes foreign shadows, and is

00:04:07.769 --> 00:04:12.930
able to soften the remainder of the facial
shadows, creating much more usable portrait

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:17.829
photos of our friends, and all this after
the photo has been made.

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:19.269
What a time to be alive!

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:24.620
Now, of course, even though this technique
convincingly beats previous works, it is still

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:25.770
not perfect.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:30.680
The algorithm may fail to remove some highly
detailed shadows, you can see how the shadow

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:34.469
of the hair remains in the output.

00:04:34.469 --> 00:04:40.020
In this other output, the hair shadows are
handled a little better, there is some dampening,

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:45.319
but the symmetric nature of the facial shadows
here put the output results in an interesting

00:04:45.319 --> 00:04:51.150
no man’s land where the opacity of the shadow
has been decreased, but the result looks unnatural.

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:56.260
I can’t wait to see how this method will
be improved two more papers down the line.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:00.800
I will be here to report on it to you, so
make sure to subscribe and hit the bell icon

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:53.919
to not miss out

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:58.059
on that.

00:05:58.059 --> 00:06:01.849
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

